## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical Azure data pipeline design with beginner-friendly constraints, testing real-world implementation of data isolation, infrastructure as code, and CI/CD practices.

## Key Concepts

- Event Hubs for scalable event ingestion
- Per-tenant data isolation through Azure SQL schemas
- PII filtering and transformation with Azure Functions
- Infrastructure as Code using Bicep
- Continuous integration/deployment with GitHub Actions
- Cross-region disaster recovery via SQL Geo-Replication and RA-GRS
- Cost optimization: autoscaling, partition configuration, retention policies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of routing logic for per-tenant data
function routeEvent(event){
  const tenantId = event.tenantId;
  // Filter PII and route to appropriate schema
  const filteredEvent = removePII(event);
  await writeToSQL(`tenant_${tenantId}`, filteredEvent);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design federated analytics with strict data residency, cross-tenant privacy, and governance in Azure at scale. It also probes edge ingestion, lakehouse patterns, federated training orchestration, and cost/DR considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Federated learning with secure aggregation and differential privacy
- Edge MQTT ingress and regional Arc gateway roles
- Delta Lake partitioning by tenant and region on ADLS Gen2
- Centralized ML registry via Azure ML; governance via Purview and BYOK
- DR with paired regions; cost controls via autoscale and quotas

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo orchestration sketch (not executable)
async function federatedStep(tenant, data) {
  ingestMQTT(tenant, data);
  streamToDelta(tenant);
  triggerFederatedTraining(tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce tenant isolation in Delta Lake at scale?
- What monitoring would you put in place for federated model drift and privacy breaches?
